    {
      "deck": "Reading Rules",
      "deck_icon": "book.closed",
      "cards": [
        {
          "q": "What is the difference in purpose between hiragana and katakana?",
          "a": "They encode the **same 46 syllables**; only the use differs.\n\n* **Hiragana** — native Japanese words, grammatical endings and particles\n* **Katakana** — loanwords, foreign names, onomatopoeia, and emphasis\n\nSo the reading you learn once serves both.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "What does the dakuten mark ( ゛) do?",
          "a": "It voices the consonant: か *ka* becomes が *ga*, さ *sa* becomes ざ *za*, た *ta* becomes だ *da*, は *ha* becomes ば *ba*.\n\nOne mark, four rows — which is why 20 of the voiced kana need no separate memorising.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "What does the handakuten mark ( ゜) do, and which row takes it?",
          "a": "It turns the **h-row only** into p: は *ha* becomes ぱ *pa*.\n\nIt is the one exception to the pattern that a diacritic voices a consonant — p is not the voiced counterpart of h, but historically the row was p.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "What does a small つ (っ) mean?",
          "a": "The **sokuon** — it doubles the following consonant and holds a beat of silence before it.\n\nきって is *kitte*, not *kite*. The pause is a full mora, so it changes the word's length as well as its spelling.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "How is a long vowel written in hiragana, and how in katakana?",
          "a": "**Hiragana** repeats the vowel kana: おおきい *ōkii*. The o-row is the irregular one — long *o* is usually written with う, as in とうきょう *Tōkyō*.\n\n**Katakana** uses a bar, ー: コーヒー *kōhī*.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "Why do the particles は, へ and を sound different from their kana?",
          "a": "As **particles** they are pronounced *wa*, *e* and *o* — not *ha*, *he* and *wo*.\n\nThe spelling preserves an older pronunciation. Inside an ordinary word the same kana read normally, so it is the grammatical role that decides.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "What does a small ゃ, ゅ or ょ do to the kana before it?",
          "a": "It merges with it into a **single mora**: き + ゃ is *kya*, one beat, not *ki-ya*, two.\n\nOnly the i-column kana (き, し, ち, に, ひ, み, り and their voiced forms) can take one.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "How many syllables does きゃ have, and why does it matter?",
          "a": "**One.** The small ゃ does not add a beat.\n\nJapanese rhythm is counted in morae, so *kyo* (one) and *kiyo* (two) are different words. Reading the small kana as a full syllable is the most common beginner's mistake.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "Which kana is written differently from how it is romanised in the s, t and h rows?",
          "a": "* し is **shi**, not *si*\n* ち is **chi**, not *ti*\n* つ is **tsu**, not *tu*\n* ふ is **fu**, not *hu*\n\nThe rows are regular in Japanese; it is the Latin alphabet that has no matching sound, which is why romanisation systems disagree here and nowhere else.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "q": "What is the gojūon, and why learn the kana in that order?",
          "a": "The traditional 5 × 10 grid — five vowels across, ten consonant rows down — that gives the syllabary its name (\"fifty sounds\").\n\nIt is the order dictionaries, textbooks and verb conjugation tables all use, so learning it makes those tables readable rather than arbitrary.",
          "a_lang": "en"
        }
      ],
      "deck_key": "reading-rules"
    }
  ]
}
